{"step_index":109,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T01:58:38Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-27T01:58:38Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-27T01:58:38Z\nShowing lines 1-70 of 281\n1: //! OSCAL subid taxonomy helpers.\n2: //!\n3: //! Every artifact in the system carries a dual identifier: a stable `uuid` and a\n4: //! human-readable `subid` operational taxonomy key. The format is mandated by\n5: //! AGENTS.md §4a:\n6: //!\n7: //! `<category>.<component-type>.<subject>.<verb>[.<facet>][@vN]`\n8: \n9: use regex::Regex;\n10: \n11: /// The seven subid categories. First segment of every subid.\n12: pub const SUBID_CATEGORIES: &[&str] = &[\"src\", \"prj\", \"sch\", \"mut\", \"obs\", \"evt\", \"exp\"];\n13: \n14: /// Component types drawn from the OSCAL standard vocabulary.\n15: ///\n16: /// This list mirrors OSCAL. Do not extend it to fit a local need — adding a\n17: /// type here is a claim about the standard. Put local types in\n18: /// [`INTERNAL_COMPONENT_TYPES`] instead.\n19: pub const OSCAL_COMPONENT_TYPES: &[&str] = &[\n20:     \"this-system\",\n21:     \"system\",\n22:     \"interconnection\",\n23:     \"software\",\n24:     \"hardware\",\n25:     \"service\",\n26:     \"policy\",\n27:     \"physical\",\n28:     \"process-procedure\",\n29:     \"plan\",\n30:     \"guidance\",\n31:     \"standard\",\n32:     \"validation\",\n33:     \"network\",\n34: ];\n35: \n36: /// Categories local to this system, deliberately kept separate from the OSCAL\n37: /// list so the standard vocabulary stays recognisable as the standard. Extend\n38: /// this one freely — that is what it is for.\n39: ///\n40: /// The internal/OSCAL split is an authoring concern, not a presentation one:\n41: /// at the render boundary these alias to plain `category`, so a UI never has to\n42: /// know which list a value came from.\n43: ///\n44: /// Each entry below is already in use by a plugin that declares or derives it.\n45: pub const INTERNAL_CATEGORIES: &[&str] = &[\n46:     \"agent\",     // persona\n47:     \"container\", // oci — `mut.container.oci.run@v1` and friends\n48:     \"data\",      // cozo\n49:     \"privacy\",   // privacy — `mut.privacy.data.mask@v1` and friends\n50:     \"security\",  // fail2ban\n51:     \"storage\",   // btrfs\n52: ];\n53: \n54: /// Category aliases: a readable name a surface may declare, paired with the\n55: /// OSCAL category it actually means.\n56: ///\n57: /// `ui` is the worked example. OSCAL has no vocabulary for \"this is a user\n58: /// interface\" — the nearest honest answer is `software` — but `software` says\n59: /// nothing useful when a dashboard is grouping surfaces for a human. So a\n60: /// plugin declares `ui`, the subid stays valid because the alias is accepted,\n61: /// and anything emitting OSCAL resolves it back to `software` via\n62: /// [`canonical_category`]. The readable name and the conformant one stay in\n63: /// sync because neither is written down twice.\n64: pub const CATEGORY_ALIASES: &[(&str, &str)] = &[\n65:     (\"ui\", \"software\"),\n66:     // Declared by `large_language_model`. A model integration is a service in\n67:     // OSCAL terms; `llm` is what it is called everywhere else in this system.\n68:     (\"llm\", \"service\"),\n69:     // Declared by the agent plugins. Distinct from the internal `agent`\n70:     // category, which `persona` uses for its own subids."}
