{"step_index":138,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T02:06:40Z","input_tokens":3093,"cache_read_tokens":111329,"output_tokens":1500,"content":"**Yes, exactly.** That is the precise split mandated by the architecture:\n\n---\n\n### The Split: Shared `threetched` vs. Instance `3tched-fs`\n\n```\n                      ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐\n                      │          SHARED (The Conglomerate)       │\n                      │                 `threetched`             │\n                      │  • Shared tools, skills & model router   │\n                      │  • Immutable catalog of capabilities     │\n                      │  • Read-only engine for all instances    │\n                      └─────────────────────┬────────────────────┘\n                                            │\n                     ┌──────────────────────┴──────────────────────┐\n                     ▼                                             ▼\n       [ Instance: Control Chatbot ]                 [ Instance: hack:3tched ]\n       `3tched-fs` (Lungs Mount 1)                   `3tched-fs` (Lungs Mount 2)\n       • Identity: Root / Superadmin                 • Identity: Subsidiary (UID 1002)\n       • Capability: Full control plane              • Capability: Scoped hackathon whitelist\n       • Call files execute as: Chatbot              • Call files execute as: `hack:3tched`\n       • Routes to: Chatbot private store            • Routes to: `hack:3tched` private store\n```\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why `threetched` Stays Shared\n* **Stateless Engine & Skills:** `threetched` exposes the external execution tools, skills (`tched_skills`), and router (`tched_router`).\n* It does not store user state or private conversations—it is simply the catalog of what the system knows how to do.\n* All subsidiaries can read from this same catalog without needing duplicate copies.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why `3tched-fs` MUST Be Instance-Specific\nIn this architecture, **the filesystem projection is the identity**:\n\n1. **Identity-Bound Call Dispatch:**\n   * When an agent writes to an operation file (e.g. `.../operations/<op>/call`), the FUSE engine identifies the caller by its mount context.\n   * `3tched-fs` stamps that write with that specific instance's session ID and UUID before dispatching to D-Bus/mutation engine.\n2. **Capability Scoping:**\n   * An instance only sees the operations and plugins it is authorized to call.\n   * `hack:3tched` gets a view containing its scoped capabilities, omitting sensitive host/root administration operations.\n3. **Private Store Anchoring:**\n   * When `3tched-fs` writes state or memory, it commits to that specific subsidiary's private ext4/Cozo store and dedicated Qdrant collection.\n\n---\n\n### Summary for `hack:3tched`\n* **`threetched`:** Points to the shared system skills and router surface.\n* **`3tched-fs`:** Mounted specifically for `/home/hack-3tched` (or inside its container) with its own `view_id`, bound to the `hack:3tched` identity sled.","thinking":"`3tchedFS` represents the filesystem, encompassing the Cargo package, plugin, and source directory. The executable and external interface is named `threetched`, derived from ZeroClaw. Host-level access distinguishes between the OP-DBUS plugins (exposed at `/run/mounts/3tchedFS`) and the `threetched` executable (exposed at `/run/mounts/threetched`).\n\n\n`threetched` represents the shared, stateless execution engine exposing universal capabilities; it's read-only and common across instances. `3tchedFS`, however, is a typed filesystem projection bound to a specific identity or container. The filesystem projection, therefore, needs to be instance-specific due to its identity association and call-file tagging within the FUSE engine.\n\n\n"}
